================================================================================
1. THE TCP ROW IS NOT A NAGLE ROW, AND IT WAS CHECKED RATHER THAN ARGUED
================================================================================

The defect found in the core's test server was that tonic ignores `tcp_nodelay`
under `serve_with_incoming`, so a 1 KB response cost MORE than a 540 KB one over
loopback TCP -- backwards for flow control, exactly right for Nagle. The
diagnostic transfers, so it was run on THIS stack, with no codec at all (the
`byte[]` passthrough both ways), at both payload sizes and both transports:

    transport            payload bytes      wall us/call
    loopback TCP                   858             133.2
    loopback TCP               540,422             905.4
    unix domain socket             858             180.2
    unix domain socket         540,422           1,196.1

**The small payload is 6.8 times CHEAPER on TCP, where the defect made it about
1.4 times dearer.** No Nagle here. The reason is that both ends of this arm are
grpc-dotnet -- Kestrel's socket transport and `SocketsHttpHandler` -- and not the
core's test server, and both set `TCP_NODELAY` by default. **So no figure this
slice has published moves**, and stage 15's finding 6 (loopback TCP slightly
FASTER than the UDS, 4,193 against 5,086 CPU us/call) is reproduced here in wall
clock at both sizes and stands.

The sharper consequence of the core's finding is taken: CPU moved as well as wall
clock there, so "CPU is the trustworthy column" holds only once the socket is
right. It is right here, so this slice's CPU columns hold -- which is a claim with
a measurement behind it now rather than an assumption.


================================================================================
2. STAGE 18'S GRID HAD AN R7 DEFECT. FIXING IT MOVES NOTHING.
================================================================================

**The defect, stated plainly.** Stage 18's cells A and D pinned ArmoniK's
transport configuration on the .NET client -- a 4 MiB stream window, dynamic
sizing off, 64 MiB message limits -- and cells B and C took TONIC'S DEFAULTS,
because the only dial the core exported was `ak_client_new`. So part of what
B-A measured could have been the settings rather than the stack, and the grid
said so nowhere.

`ak_client_new_opts` closes it. The core client is now pinned to what the .NET
client does, because the grid varies the CLIENT: stream window 4 MiB, connection
window 64 MiB (which `Http2Connection` hardcodes, established from the runtime
source in stage 15), adaptive sizing OFF, 64 MiB message limits, Nagle off --
which is what `armonik-transport` ships. The unpinned cells are KEPT as B* and
C* so the correction is visible rather than silently replacing the number.

B/A, the same codec over each transport, CPU per call:

    in flight                     1          8         16
    B  pinned, forward        0.823      0.832      0.548
    B  pinned, reversed       0.812      0.852      0.536
    B* tonic defaults, fwd    0.831      0.840      0.629
    B* tonic defaults, rev    0.805      0.852      0.495

**Pinning moves nothing outside the spreads, and at 16 in flight the unpinned
client is slightly the faster of the two in one order and slower in the other.**
So the defect was real methodology and an immaterial number, which is worth
saying in exactly that order.

**Stage 18's headline survives its own defect, and tightens.** Across four runs
(two settings, two orders) the core's transport costs **18 to 19 percent less CPU
per call at 1 in flight, 15 to 17 percent at 8, and 45 to 46 percent at 16**.
Stage 18 reported 12 to 44 percent from three runs; these four agree with it and
narrow it.

**And the question the grid exists for is still not answerable.** C-B and D-A,
the codec difference under each transport, across both orders:

    in flight     C-B forward   C-B reversed   D-A forward   D-A reversed
    1                   -33.3         -266.7        -133.3         -333.3
    8                  +168.9         +371.6        -304.1         +405.4
    16                 +625.0         -277.8        -138.9        -1145.8

Both still change SIGN with the cell order and both still sit inside the
round-to-round spreads (2 to 26 percent). |B-A| is 709 to 3,576 us a call;
neither codec subtraction reaches 1,146. **The transport half is three to ten
times the codec half and the codec half is at this harness's noise floor** --
unchanged from stage 18, now with the R7 objection removed as an explanation.


================================================================================
3. THE TRANSPORT'S CROSSINGS, COUNTED. SECTION 9'S "TWO PER CALL" IS THE
   BLOCKING MODE'S COUNT AND THE OTHER TWO COST FOUR.
================================================================================

The core now exports `ak_rpc_counters`, live only in a build carrying `count`
beside `rpc`, with `ak_rpc_counting()` to say which build the host is holding.
Both builds exist here and the harness REFUSES to print a count from the
non-counting one, because a zero there means "not measured" and not "free".

200 calls per delivery, P2.2 (200 elements of a 27-field message):

    delivery     forward/call   reverse/call   total
    blocking             2.00           0.00       2
    callback             3.00           1.00       4
    queue                4.00           0.00       4

**Two per call is the blocking mode**: the call in, and `ak_bytes_free` out.
The callback delivery costs FOUR -- `ak_call_unary_cb`, `ak_call_destroy`,
`ak_bytes_free`, and the completion upcall -- and the queue costs four as well,
with `ak_queue_next` in place of the upcall and no reverse crossing at all. The
handle that makes a call cancellable is what the extra forward crossings buy.

**None of them varies with the payload.** P2.2 carries thousands of fields and
the counts are exactly 2, 4 and 4, so "the RPC half does not know the schema"
is a reading here rather than an assertion.

**What it costs: nothing.** Two extra crossings at this slice's own calibrated
7.5 to 12 ns is 15 to 24 ns on a call of about 4,000 us -- four to six parts per
million. So the correction is to the CLAIM and not to any number: section 9
should say two crossings for the blocking form and four for each non-blocking
one, rather than two for the family.


================================================================================
4. A SMALL CORRECTION TO THE HAND-OFF
================================================================================

`ak_client_opts` has SIX fields, not the five relayed: `tcp_nagle` (1 on, 0 off,
-1 leave tonic's default, which is nodelay on) is the sixth, and it is the field
the Nagle finding added. A host binding the five would have got a struct one
word short of the core's and read garbage into it. Found by reading the struct
rather than the message.


================================================================================
WHAT IS NOT MEASURED
================================================================================

  * Whether C-B and D-A agree. Two more orders did not settle it and more rounds
    will not settle a difference that changes sign with cell order.
  * The upstream direction, streaming over the core's transport, cancellation,
    deadlines, metadata, status codes, TLS.
  * More than one queue drainer.
  * The crossing counts under concurrency: the count run is sequential, so it
    establishes the per-call shape and not a contended one.
  * Absolutes. R13: this container's numbers are instrumentation.


